  "worked_solution": "Using the fact that $6=\\frac{6}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{3-2k}{k}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $k$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{k}{k}\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{6k}{k}+\\frac{3-2k}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6k}{k}+\\frac{3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{6k+3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{4k+3}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
